---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Remorch admin panel: the upgrade was the cheap part"
  description="Moving a live admin panel three Angular majors forward and zoneless, where the real work was 53 components that repainted by luck."
  path="/work/remorch-panel"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">Index</a>

  <h1 class="lede">The upgrade was the cheap part</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    The panel the desk uses for orders, customers and payouts. Three Angular majors in one pass,
    plus zoneless and a new grid major.
  </p>

  <dl class="dossier">
    <div>
      <dt>Surface</dt>
      <dd>Internal admin: orders, customers, partners, financial, support</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Work</dt>
      <dd>Angular 19 to 22, zoneless, ag-grid 33 to 36, standalone, test tooling</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Stack</dt>
      <dd>Angular 22, ag-grid 36, ECharts, vitest</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <section class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/remorch-panel/financial-dashboard.webp"
        alt="The financial dashboard with a weekly revenue bar chart and a wallet balance card"
        width="1034"
        height="689"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>The financial dashboard.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What actually cost time</h2>
    <p>
      Version bumps are mostly mechanical, so the plan started by ranking the risks. Zoneless came first:
      53 components changed how they detect changes at once, about 16 of them only repainted by
      luck, and the code had no explicit change-detector calls to fall back on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second was the grid's own major, which folds nine or more scroll containers into one and
      renames layout classes that four of our style overrides depended on. The TypeScript major
      turned out to be a non-event. Two dependencies weren't imported anywhere, and deleting them
      saved dragging an error-reporting library through its own upgrade.
    </p>

    <h2>Same library, opposite call</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/work/remorch-pay">payment page</a> dropped its component library. This panel
      kept it on the free tier. The payment page is public and used six components, so removal took
      an afternoon. The panel is internal and built from that library on every screen, so keeping it
      made more sense.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking it, and one objection</h2>
    <p>
      About twenty routes were compared before and after, with accessibility checks in the same
      run, against a read-only proxy so the data had real shapes. The test runner was replaced in
      the same pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      The decision was to deliver it as one squashed commit. I argued against that and lost, so I wrote the
      tradeoff into the plan: a grid regression, a repaint bug and a compile error would all land in
      one diff, with no CI to tell them apart.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/remorch-panel/orders-filters.webp"
        alt="The all-orders grid with type tabs and an open filter panel, with the username column blurred"
        width="1920"
        height="1080"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The orders grid with filters open. Usernames are blurred because this is production data.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <p><a class="action" href="/work/remorch-app">The wallet, and a signature that went missing</a></p>
</Base>
